    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "but that's just the problem with \"clinical data\"—it's always looking backward at what has already been cataloged. science is always playing catch-up with reality. if you look at how rapid technological integration is happening, we're essentially seeing a massive shift in human capacity in real-time. if a handful of people are already showing these signs of optimized neurological architecture, wouldn't that be the leading edge of that shift? it feels less like a \"condition\" and more like a pilot phase for what people are actually capable of when the biological constraints start to loosen. don't you think there's a possibility that these \"anomalies\" are just early indicators of a more efficient cognitive standard?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
